    def whoami(self, input: Optional[T.WhoamiInput] = None, **opts: Unpack[RequestOptions]) -> T.WhoamiOutput:
        """Get the caller's resolved identity

        Who you are talking to. Answers from the gateway's resolved context, so it works for every credential a caller can arrive with — a dashboard JWT, an API key, an embed (widget) token and a share link alike; unlike GET /api/v1/profile, which reads the Keycloak user and is therefore unavailable to an embed session. Call it before your first substantive reply and adapt to the answer. Two independent axes, and do not collapse them: `actor` is WHICH CREDENTIAL, `actingAsTenant` is WHOSE WORKSPACE. `actingAsTenant: true` means the credential was minted for a tenant of a parent org — `org` is that customer's own workspace and `parentOrg` is the partner whose product they are using — so speak in business terms about their own connections and not about building the platform. Their provider's integrations are theirs to use, and to customise by forking a copy into their own workspace (forkIntegration) - never in place. It is true for a partner's customer in an embedded widget AND for a tenant-scoped API key, so it does not on its own mean a human is present; read `actor` for that. `actingAsTenant: false` means the caller is acting as the org itself, which for `actor: user` is the partner's own staff in the dashboard, with `role` naming the persona. `permissions` reports three layers rather than one merged list, because enforcement is a layered predicate and a merged copy would drift from it: `role` is the enforced baseline for the persona, `structured` overrides it when present (for a custom-role member it IS the effective set, already intersected against the base role), and `apiKeyGrant` is a ceiling over both and never widens them. Retired-resource entries may appear in `role` and govern nothing. Every tool call you make executes under this identity, so a 403 means this persona lacks the permission rather than a fault worth retrying. One limitation: a caller whose token carries no org claim at all is answered 401 rather than a partial identity, which in practice means a platform_admin holding no org_id — the same guard every other /api/v1/me/* read uses.
        GET /v1/me
        """
        return cast('T.WhoamiOutput', self._t.call(OPS['whoami'], input or {}, cast(Mapping[str, Any], opts)))


